::ENG4_U02_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: When the council gathered to vote on closing the old reading room, Elias sat in the back beside the empty chair his mother had used every Thursday. The room smelled of paper, dust, and raincoats. On the wall, a cracked photograph showed children holding library cards like small flags. Several council members argued that the building was inefficient and that a digital archive would serve more people. Elias agreed that the town needed better access, yet he kept looking at the empty chair. When his turn came to speak, he did not ask the council to preserve every shelf. Instead, he asked them to carry the reading room's promise into whatever came next: a place where no child had to enter knowledge alone. Which is a theme, not just a topic? {
~A. Digital archive#This is a topic.
=B. Meaningful change should preserve the human purpose of the past.#Correct. This is a thematic insight.
~C. Empty chair#This is a symbol or object.
~D. Council meeting#This is an event.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Elias sat in the back beside the empty chair his mother had used every Thursday. What theme begins developing through this detail? {
=A. Personal memory can shape public responsibility.#Correct. The empty chair connects private memory to public action.
~B. Technology is always harmful.#This is not supported.
~C. Buildings never change.#The building may close.
~D. Reading rooms are physically dangerous.#This is not supported.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Several council members argued that the building was inefficient and that a digital archive would serve more people. Which theme develops through this argument? {
~A. Communities should never use technology.#This contradicts the argument.
~B. Old buildings are always useless.#This is too absolute.
=C. Change can expand access when resources are limited.#Correct. The council's argument introduces access through change.
~D. Children should not read.#This is not supported.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Elias agreed that the town needed better access, yet he kept looking at the empty chair. How does this sentence develop multiple themes? {
~A. It shows Elias wants to end all access.#This contradicts the sentence.
~B. It proves Elias cannot remember his mother.#He looks at her chair.
~C. It removes conflict from the passage.#It complicates conflict.
=D. It shows Elias values access while still feeling the pull of memory.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Passage: Elias sat in the back beside the empty chair his mother had used every Thursday. What is the functional significance of the empty chair? {
~A. It proves the room is full.#The chair is empty.
=B. It symbolizes personal memory and absence.#Correct
~C. It identifies the mayor.#No mayor is mentioned.
~D. It explains how digital archives work.#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Several council members argued that the building was inefficient and that a digital archive would serve more people. What does archive mean? {
=A. A collection of stored records or materials#Correct
~B. A broken chair#This is unrelated.
~C. A public speech#This is not an archive.
~D. A weather pattern#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: He did not ask the council to preserve every shelf. Instead, he asked them to carry the reading room's promise into whatever came next. What does the ending show about theme interaction? {
~A. Elias rejects all compromise.#He offers a compromise.
~B. The council leaves immediately.#This is not stated.
~C. The reading room never mattered.#The passage shows it mattered.
=D. Memory and change can work together when purpose is preserved.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The room smelled of paper, dust, and raincoats. On the wall, a cracked photograph showed children holding library cards like small flags. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. The room is brand new and empty.#This contradicts the details.
~B. The children are physically waving national flags.#The phrase is a simile.
=C. The room contains signs of age and a photograph of children with library cards.#Correct
~D. The photograph shows council members arguing.#This is not stated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Passage: On the wall, a cracked photograph showed children holding library cards like small flags. What does the simile suggest? {
=A. Library cards represent belonging, pride, or possibility.#Correct
~B. The children are in a military parade.#This literalizes the simile.
~C. Library cards are dangerous.#This is not suggested.
~D. The photograph is irrelevant.#The detail supports theme.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: The room smelled of paper, dust, and raincoats. Which theme does this sensory detail support most directly? {
~A. Technology is impossible.#This is not supported.
~B. Weather controls the council.#Raincoats are a sensory detail, not the theme.
~C. Children dislike books.#This is not stated.
=D. The past remains present through physical spaces.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Several council members argued that the building was inefficient and that a digital archive would serve more people. Elias agreed that the town needed better access, yet he kept looking at the empty chair. Which statement best evaluates Elias's position? {
~A. He refuses to listen to practical concerns.#He agrees access is needed.
=B. He balances practical access with emotional and communal memory.#Correct
~C. He cares only about technology.#He also cares about the chair and memory.
~D. He wants children to enter knowledge alone.#He says the opposite.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: He asked them to carry the reading room's promise into whatever came next. What does promise mean in this context? {
~A. A written contract about rent#This is not the meaning here.
~B. A broken shelf#This is unrelated.
=C. The room's deeper purpose or commitment#Correct
~D. A weather prediction#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: He did not ask the council to preserve every shelf. Instead, he asked them to carry the reading room's promise into whatever came next. Which theme is developed by this contrast? {
=A. Preserving values can matter more than preserving every physical object.#Correct
~B. Shelves are the only important part of a library.#The sentence rejects that idea.
~C. The council should stop listening.#This is not supported.
~D. Digital access has no value.#Elias supports better access.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Elias agreed that the town needed better access, yet he kept looking at the empty chair. Which paraphrase preserves the contrast? {
~A. Elias denied that access mattered.#This contradicts the sentence.
=B. Elias recognized the need for access but remained emotionally connected to the reading room's past.#Correct
~C. Elias forgot the empty chair.#This contradicts the sentence.
~D. Elias demanded that every shelf be preserved.#This is not stated in this sentence.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: A cracked photograph showed children holding library cards like small flags. How does this detail develop a theme? {
~A. It proves all photographs are damaged.#This is too broad.
~B. It shows the room should be emptied immediately.#This is not supported.
~C. It removes children from the passage.#The children are central to the image.
=D. It suggests access to knowledge can give children dignity and belonging.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Passage: The room smelled of paper, dust, and raincoats. What is the function of this imagery? {
~A. It proves the room is outdoors.#The room is indoors.
~B. It explains digital storage.#This is unrelated.
=C. It makes the reading room feel physically present and historically worn.#Correct
~D. It creates a comic tone.#The tone is reflective.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: When his turn came to speak, he did not ask the council to preserve every shelf. Instead, he asked them to carry the reading room's promise into whatever came next. Which evaluation is strongest? {
=A. The ending develops both themes by transforming the conflict into a compromise grounded in purpose.#Correct
~B. The ending proves the reading room has no value.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. The ending ignores the digital archive completely.#It responds to change.
~D. The ending is only about furniture.#It is about purpose.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Elias agreed that the town needed better access. What does access mean in this context? {
~A. A cracked photograph#This is unrelated.
~B. A type of chair#This is unrelated.
~C. A smell in a room#This is unrelated.
=D. The ability to reach or use something#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Several council members argued that the building was inefficient and that a digital archive would serve more people. Which evidence best supports the theme of adaptation? {
~A. The chair was empty.#This supports memory more directly.
=B. The digital archive would serve more people.#Correct
~C. The room smelled of paper.#This supports the past.
~D. The photograph was cracked.#This supports history and memory.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Elias sat in the back beside the empty chair his mother had used every Thursday. Which evidence best supports the theme of honoring the past? {
=A. Elias sits beside his mother's empty chair.#Correct
~B. A digital archive would serve more people.#This supports adaptation.
~C. The building was inefficient.#This supports the argument for change.
~D. The council gathered to vote.#This is plot context.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.01] Passage: The empty chair his mother had used every Thursday. What does the detail every Thursday add? {
~A. It proves the meeting is on Thursday.#That is not stated.
~B. It makes the chair unimportant.#It does the opposite.
=C. It shows the chair represents a repeated, meaningful habit.#Correct
~D. It removes Elias from the scene.#Elias remains central.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: He asked them to carry the reading room's promise into whatever came next: a place where no child had to enter knowledge alone. What final theme does this line develop? {
~A. Children should never read alone because books are unsafe.#This distorts the line.
=B. Knowledge access should include guidance and belonging.#Correct
~C. The reading room's only purpose was furniture storage.#This is not supported.
~D. The council has already voted.#The vote outcome is not stated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Several council members argued that the building was inefficient and that a digital archive would serve more people. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Council members said the building was perfect and should never change.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Elias argued that digital archives harm everyone.#This is not stated.
~C. The children voted to close the reading room.#This is not stated.
=D. Some council members said the building used resources poorly and that digital access could reach more people.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Elias agreed that the town needed better access, yet he kept looking at the empty chair. Why is yet important? {
=A. It signals tension between practical change and emotional memory.#Correct
~B. It means Elias leaves the room.#This is not stated.
~C. It proves the council is silent.#This is not stated.
~D. It removes the theme of access.#The sentence includes access.
}
::ENG4_U02_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Passage: Instead, he asked them to carry the reading room's promise into whatever came next: a place where no child had to enter knowledge alone. Which statement best evaluates the passage's theme development? {
~A. The passage says the past should always defeat the future.#This misses the synthesis.
~B. The passage says technology matters more than people.#This contradicts the final line.
=C. The passage moves from a conflict between preservation and change toward a synthesis of memory, access, and shared purpose.#Correct
~D. The passage has no theme because it includes a council meeting.#This is incorrect.
}
